  "grammar": {
    "title": "Step III: Grammar Blueprints — Negative & Restrictive Inversions",
    "pill": "Core • C1/C2 Syntactic Blueprints & 8 Part 4 Transformations",
    "introHtml": "When a negative or restrictive adverbial is placed at the very start of a clause for emphasis, English flips the auxiliary or modal verb before the subject—just like a question:",
    "pillars": [
      {
        "heading": "1. Frequency & Cognitive Negatives (Seldom / Never / Little / At no time)",
        "bodyHtml": "Formula: <code>[Seldom / Never before / Little / At no time] + AUX (did/had/have) + Subject + Bare Infinitive / V3</code>.",
        "exampleHtml": "• <em><strong>Seldom have</strong> psychological thrillers matched Poe's pacing.</em><br>• <em><strong>Little did the officers suspect</strong> what lay beneath the planks.</em>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "2. Consecutive Past Time (Scarcely... when / Hardly... when / No sooner... than)",
        "bodyHtml": "Formula: <code>[Scarcely / Hardly] + HAD + Subject + V3 ... WHEN</code> vs. <code>[No sooner] + HAD + Subject + V3 ... THAN</code>.",
        "exampleHtml": "• <em><strong>Scarcely had</strong> my thumb touched the lantern <strong>when</strong> the old man cried out.</em><br>• <em><strong>No sooner had</strong> I sat down <strong>than</strong> the ticking began.</em>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "3. Delayed Restrictive Inversion (Not until / Only when)",
        "bodyHtml": "Formula: <code>[Not until / Only when + Normal Clause] + AUX (did/had) + Subject + Bare Verb</code>. Never invert the first clause!",
        "exampleHtml": "• <em><strong>Not until</strong> the eighth night <strong>did he lose</strong> his composure.</em><br>• <em><strong>Only when</strong> they smiled <strong>did I recognise</strong> their hypocrisy.</em>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "4. Prohibition & Unreal Preference (Under no circumstances / Would rather)",
        "bodyHtml": "Formula: <code>[Under no circumstances] + Modal/Aux + S + V1</code> | <code>Subject + would rather + [Other Subject] + Past Simple / Past Perfect</code>.",
        "exampleHtml": "• <em><strong>Under no circumstances could I endure</strong> their derision.</em><br>• <em>I <strong>would rather they had left</strong> before the ringing grew loud.</em>"
      }
    ],
    "mcqs": [
      {
        "number": 13,
        "promptHtml": "Which sentence accurately pairs a negative time inversion with its mandatory correlative conjunction?",
        "correctIdx": 1,
        "options": [
          {
            "textHtml": "A) Scarcely had the police officers entered the room than the muffled ticking began.",
            "feedback": "Not quite: 'Scarcely' and 'Hardly' must pair with 'when' ('than' belongs exclusively to 'No sooner')."
          },
          {
            "textHtml": "B) No sooner had the police officers entered the room than the muffled ticking began.",
            "feedback": "Spot on! 'No sooner + had + Subject + V3 ... than' is the exact C1/C2 formula."
          },
          {
            "textHtml": "C) Hardly the police officers had entered the room when the muffled ticking began.",
            "feedback": "Not quite: Fronting 'Hardly' requires auxiliary inversion ('Hardly had the police officers entered...')."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "number": 14,
        "promptHtml": "Where does auxiliary inversion take place when a sentence opens with <em>Only when</em> or <em>Not until</em>?",
        "correctIdx": 2,
        "options": [
          {
            "textHtml": "A) Immediately after 'Only when': <em>'Only when did the officers smile I recognised their mockery.'</em>",
            "feedback": "Not quite: Never invert the subordinate time clause after 'Only when' or 'Not until'."
          },
          {
            "textHtml": "B) In both clauses: <em>'Only when did the officers smile did I recognise their mockery.'</em>",
            "feedback": "Not quite: Double inversion is ungrammatical."
          },
          {
            "textHtml": "C) Delayed until the main clause: <em>'Only when the officers smiled did I recognise their mockery.'</em>",
            "feedback": "Exactly right! The time clause ('the officers smiled') stays in normal word order, and the main clause inverts ('did I recognise')."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "number": 15,
        "promptHtml": "Which sentence uses a negative prepositional opener (<em>At no time / Under no circumstances</em>) accurately?",
        "correctIdx": 0,
        "options": [
          {
            "textHtml": "A) At no time during the week before the murder did the narrator speak unkindly to the old man.",
            "feedback": "Correct! 'At no time... did the narrator speak' places the auxiliary 'did' before the subject and keeps 'speak' in the bare infinitive."
          },
          {
            "textHtml": "B) At no time during the week before the murder did the narrator spoke unkindly to the old man.",
            "feedback": "Not quite: After auxiliary 'did', the main verb must be the bare infinitive ('speak'), never past simple ('spoke')."
          },
          {
            "textHtml": "C) Under no circumstances the narrator could endure the relentless beating of the heart.",
            "feedback": "Not quite: 'Under no circumstances' requires immediate modal inversion ('could the narrator endure')."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
